  "content": "# Why Database Indexes Speed Up Reads but Slow Down Writes\n\n## The Core Idea\n\nAn index is a **separate data structure** (usually a B-tree or hash table) that maps column values to the physical locations of rows. It's like the index at the back of a textbook: instead of scanning every page to find a topic, you look it up alphabetically and jump straight to the right page.\n\nThat extra structure is what creates the trade-off.\n\n---\n\n## Why Reads Get Faster\n\nWithout an index, a query like `SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'a@b.com'` forces a **full table scan** — the database reads every row until it finds a match. That's O(n).\n\nWith an index on `email`, the database walks a B-tree in O(log n) steps and lands directly on the matching row(s). For a table with 10 million rows, that's roughly 23 comparisons instead of 10 million.\n\n---\n\n## Why Writes Get Slower\n\nEvery `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, or `DELETE` must keep **every index on the table consistent** with the data. That means:\n\n1. **INSERT** — the new row must be added to the table *and* inserted into every index",
